<!--Post: 13279--><!--Time: 1114766813-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13279"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Itty<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Itty<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, April 29, 2005 - 2:26 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->BTK:  MY PEN PAL <BR>  <BR>It&#39;s a claim a west coast woman can make after receiving a four page letter from Dennis Rader from his jail cell here in Wichita.  The woman thought that she would send him a letter, and did not know what to expect.  She was floored when she got a response, and that it was so long.  In the letter, he talks about how much he has relied upon his pastor and the jail&#39;s chaplain.  He also talks about his daily routine, and expresses that receiving any mail &#34;expressing Christian concerns for him and his family&#34; is a welcome perk. <BR> <BR><a href="http://www.wichitanewsbrief.com/newsletter.aspx?id=recent" target=_top>http://www.wichitanewsbrief.com/newsletter.aspx?id =recent</a><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13279-->
<!--Post: 13286--><!--Time: 1114792360-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13286"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Beck<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Beck<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, April 29, 2005 - 9:32 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->This is the same letter that Tom posted the excerpt from. There is a story about it on KAKE, and they even feature catchbtk.com <BR> <BR><a href="http://www.kake.com/home/headlines/1526147.html" target=_top>http://www.kake.com/home/headlines/1526147.html</a><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13286-->
<!--Post: 13290--><!--Time: 1114811401-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13290"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, April 29, 2005 - 2:50 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Serial murderers tend to respond to letters from civilians. The perpetrator spends a considerable amount of their time isolated or with other undesirables and welcome the opportunity to correspond. The serial murderer is more likely to respond when the approach expresses compassion and empathy. It provides the perpetrator with an opportunity to exert influence and control on another individual. <BR> <BR>I have communicated with serial murderers and maintain dialogue with one at the moment. The responses tend to be 5 to 6 pages in length. The perpetrator is initially curious and will attempt to assert control and domination. If the perpetrator believes they are not being exploited they actually look forward to the communication and are willing to discuss matters related to their life, relationships, family, interests and experience in prison.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13290-->
<!--Post: 13295--><!--Time: 1114820981-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13295"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Rocky<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Rocky<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, April 29, 2005 - 5:29 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Ivy - you correspond with serial killers? Interesting! Tell us more. Do you show compassion and empathy to gain trust? Are you going to write a book, thesis, journal etc... based on your experience?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13295-->
<!--Post: 13298--><!--Time: 1114823983-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13298"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, April 29, 2005 - 6:19 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I commenced communicating with a serial murderer from California in the late 1990s and have contacted several during the last five years in Illinois, Connnecticut and Alaska. I was motivated by the desire to understand the psychology of their crimes and the sexual gratification gained from murder.  <BR> <BR>I conduct research on the serial murderer before corresponding with them. The research involves consulting academic literature, psychiatric reports when available and true crime literature. This tends to last for a period of six months. It provides an insight into the perpetrator and how to approach him. <BR> <BR>The expression of compassion and empathy does not imply an endorsement of their crimes. It refers to an understanding of the boredom, isolation and drudgery of prison life. In the first letter, I invite the perpetrator to respond. This allows them to assume the dominant role during the initial stage of the relationship. During the course of the dialogue their control tends to diminish. This can be attributed to several factors. They have difficulty believing I would be interested in their case, but after providing some interpretation and analysis they are interested in corresponding. The communication can involve writing a letter every week to the perpetrator. <BR> <BR>I have no desire at the moment to publish a book, journal or thesis. Jason Moss wrote a book detailing his communication with Richard Ramirez, John Wayne Gacy, Charles Manson and Jeffrey Dahmer. Ramirez was not impressed by the book and harassed him.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13298-->
